flammable, non-pajama-looking, non-comfy denim set.

282
00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:57.840
We don't know they're not comfy yet.

283
00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:58.320
That's true.

284
00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:58.880
That's true.

285
00:08:58.880 --> 00:08:59.940
All right, we'll be back.

286
00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:17.680
We are back, and I have to apologize a little bit to Valuna because I am very comfortable

287
00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:18.280
right now.

288
00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:18.980
I am too.

289
00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.400
And in a photograph, I think I'm looking good.

290
00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:29.240
In person, I look like a cray-cray because I'm wearing what looks like—

291
00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:29.880
It's a print.

292
00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.180
—a Halloween costume.

293
00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:32.220
It looks like a Halloween costume.

294
00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:33.820
It does photograph well.

295
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.900
Also, the pants do have pockets.

296
00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:36.460
Yeah.

297
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:37.440
We thought they didn't.

298
00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:37.840
Yeah.

299
00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:38.280
Yeah.

300
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:39.720
And you know what?

301
00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:40.900
We're ready for a video call.

302
00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.100
We—we—because it does look good on camera.

303
00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:44.640
It does.

304
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:45.540
All right, everyone.

305
00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:51.000
Let's go ahead and get into some of the most expensive props, episodes, and moments from

306
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.760
our nine years on The Office.

307
00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.780
But before we dive into those, we thought it might be a good idea to get an idea of exactly

308
00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.680
how our show budgets worked.

309
00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:03.000
Yes, you need something to compare these expensive moments to.

310
00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:04.560
Like, what's the scale here?

311
00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.260
I need to change out of this outfit.

312
00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:06.820
Oh.

313
00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.340
I'm starting to sweat.

314
00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:09.000
Oh.

315
00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.120
And the heat—

316
00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.540
It holds in heat.

317
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:13.740
This is not for me.

318
00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:15.880
It is 95% polyester.

319
00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.020
I'm going to need a moment, everyone.

320
00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.020
I lasted—how long did this last?

321
00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:22.460
Not five minutes.

322
00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.320
Since I put it on my body.

323
00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:25.420
I think it was like two minutes.

324
00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.720
It looked like, in the picture, like that soft, soft denim.

325
00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.100
I was very excited.

326
00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:30.540
Yeah.

327
00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:31.140
Okay.

328
00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:31.700
Okay.

329
00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.400
Oh, Jenna's coming back.

330
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:36.960
You're in your cotton shirt.

331
00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:37.420
Yes.

332
00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:38.780
Your breathable pants.

333
00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:40.760
Let's get into budgets.

334
00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:46.060
A big thanks to Steve Burgess, who answered our question about budgets.

335
00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:52.000
He said, we had both a per-episode and a per-season budget.

336
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:58.920
But he said the per-season budget was basically just totaling up the episode budgets.

337
00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.740
But you could steal.

338
00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:07.500
So, like, if you went over on one, you could go under on another, and you could balance the books at the end of the season.

339
00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:13.360
And if they were going to go over budget, the universal executives had to approve it.

340
00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:17.460
He said they really, really, really wanted them to stay in budget.

341
00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.080
Yeah.

342
00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:18.980
It was stressful.

343
00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:30.920
And if they were going to go over, that overage had to be approved before we started shooting an episode, which Steve Burgess says you might remember,

344
00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.080
Greg was often rewriting over the weekend into Monday.

345
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:40.440
I was going to say, how can you pre-approve a budget that might change because scenes change?

346
00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:40.880
Yeah.

347
00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:46.740
He said, sometimes we had already been shooting for a day or two, and NBC would get really mad.

348
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:47.460
Oh.

349
00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:48.080
Yeah.

350
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:48.880
They don't like it.

351
00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.440
He actually wrote, they hated it.

352
00:11:53.200 --> 00:12:02.520
He actually said, I'm surprised that Pam Putch, who was their NBC executive, didn't pull all our hair out because we were a very difficult group for NBC to control.

353
00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:10.400
But he did say that they always did their best to be as responsible as possible about the money they spent.

354
00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.100
I did love that he said, NBC didn't yell too much.

355
00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:16.600
Winky face.

356
00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.440
We also heard from Kent Zabornak, like Jenna said, our beloved Kentopedia.

357
00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:27.160
He was our line producer and executive producer for seasons one through four.

358
00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:31.380
And we asked him what the most expensive episodes were from his time on The Office.

359
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.660
You might remember he shared with us how very small our budget was in the beginning, like crazy small.

360
00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:38.120
Yeah.

361
00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.100
I mean, we were barely picked up.

362
00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:40.380
Yeah.

363
00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:43.020
So they weren't sinking a lot of money into our show.

364
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:49.880
And since Kent was with us in the beginning, I was really trying to think of when the show might have gotten expensive.

365
00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.840
Because I know we were shoestring budget for a long time.

366
00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:54.180
Right.

367
00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.600
And we both decided to make a list and guess.

368
00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.140
So what do you think were the most expensive from the early years?

369
00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:05.260
So my guesses were Goodbye Toby or Beach Games.

370
00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:05.900
Yeah.

371
00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:07.900
Beach Games seems like an expensive one.

372
00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:08.260
Yeah.

373
00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:11.140
And I thought Goodbye Toby because of the whole outdoor carnival.

374
00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:11.780
Oh, right.

375
00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.000
Well, I guessed Booze Cruise.

376
00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:14.440
Mm-hmm.

377
00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:16.020
And I only had one on my liSt.

378
00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:17.300
I just went with Booze Cruise.

379
00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:19.520
Kent Zabornak did confirm that.

380
00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:20.840
And here's what he had to say.

381
00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:26.380
He said in his four seasons on The Office, the most costly episode was Booze Cruise.

382
00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:27.620
He put in all caps.

383
00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:34.940
In addition to being on location in Long Beach on the water for three days, they had to pay for an entire additional crew,

384
00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:41.500
which consisted of a water coordinator and his crew of 20 sailors and lifeguards.

385
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.340
Add that to the cost of all the boat rentals, there were a total of 15.

386
00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.680
I didn't even see any of this stuff.

387
00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:49.200
I know.

388
00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:50.200
I mean, I remember—

389
00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:50.920
This was not on my radar.

390
00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:51.500
Right.

391
00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:56.860
I remember the big boat and, of course, the little dinghy boats that I got stranded in.

392
00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:58.060
Let's not forget that part.

393
00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:03.740
He said there was also the cost of the fuel, plus all the film permits from the city of Long Beach.

394
00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:09.860
And then the cast and crew had hotels per diem because they didn't want people driving long distances at night.

395
00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:12.180
And then we had all the guest caSt.

396
00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:18.400
David Dinman, Amy Adams, Rob Riggle, Brenda Withers, all the day players, the band, the Scrantones.

397
00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:25.180
And then all of the crew hit premium golden hours pay because they were working such late shifts.

398
00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:26.360
Oh, yeah.

399
00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.720
That's when you get, like, double, triple time.

400
00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:29.060
Yeah.

401
00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:31.140
They did that three days on this shoot.

402
00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:31.700
Wow.

403
00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:38.900
And then, of course, don't forget all the little extras, like the warm heaters that we had, the jackets, all of those things.

404
00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:40.380
Yeah.

405
00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.040
I think about that all the time.

406
00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:48.000
Whenever we were doing something where we were cold, like, especially booze crews was kind of the first one.

407
00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.800
The wardrobe department had, like, warm boots.

408
00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.460
They had hand warmers.

409
00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.280
They had a big coat for each one of us.

410
00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:56.740
It had our character name in it.

411
00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:57.020
Yeah.

412
00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:57.940
They were huge.

413
00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.500
Like, they look like sleeping bags.

414
00:14:59.680 --> 00:14:59.880
Yes.

415
00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:01.040
But they were a coat.

416
00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:07.100
And if you've ever had to buy those, like, hand warmers and toe warmers, those are expensive.

417
00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:09.360
And they would buy those for the whole caSt.

418
00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:10.100
And crew.

419
00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:10.560
Yes.

420
00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:11.480
Everyone had them.

421
00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:11.760
Yeah.

422
00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:12.340
Yes.

423
00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:17.400
Well, something else I remember from booze crews is that boat was really bare bones.

424
00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:19.220
Like, Kent sent me photos of it.

425
00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.940
And my memory is that it was just like a shell.

426
00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.320
I don't even think there were those booths.

427
00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:31.060
Don't quote me, but I feel like we had to add all those elements for our shoot.

428
00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:31.860
I had no idea.

429
00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.140
I thought that boat came with all those seats and stuff.

430
00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:36.200
I don't think so.

431
00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:37.720
We had to create a dance floor.

432
00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:38.620
I remember that.

433
00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.440
We had to do the fakie wheel that Dwight uses on the front of the boat.

434
00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:42.800
Yeah.

435
00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:48.720
And, you know, when we originally broke down this episode for the podcast, Greg Daniels joined us in the studio.

436
00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:51.080
And we talked about all kinds of amazing elements.

437
00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:54.220
But I did ask him about one particular shot.

438
00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:58.980
It's from outside the boat, looking back, you know, onto the ship.

439
00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:04.540
And it brought him to this great little share about Kent's ability to stretch a budget.

440
00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:05.300
Let's hear it.

441
00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:07.620
I have a question for you, Greg.

442
00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:07.900
Yeah.

443
00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.720
I've been waiting to ask you this.

444
00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:17.660
At 11 minutes, 34 seconds, there's this beautiful shot that goes outside of the boat and goes around the boat.

445
00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:22.200
You pass Dwight, right, like steering, and it goes around the boat.

446
00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:23.140
How did we do that?

447
00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:24.980
That is Kent Zabornak.

448
00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:29.480
So Kent was our line producer, and he was also in the DGA.

449
00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:40.320
And he managed to launch us on the budget that we had, which was tiny, into Long Beach Harbor on a boat for days and get hotel rooms for everybody.

450
00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:44.940
And he realized that we really needed a shot like that.

451
00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:54.980
And he shot that himself with his own equipment while we were shooting the rest of it because he just had an instinct that there was no way the production could get that and stop everything.

452
00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:58.600
So that was kind of a guerrilla move and super valuable.

453
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.600
And Kent was, you know, fantastic at doing those kind of things.

454
00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:08.120
Like he was always stitching together amazing stuff on our little budget.

455
00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:10.260
I remember that story.

456
00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:10.820
Yeah.

457
00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.980
So the tiny budget of those early years, Kent had to get really creative with.

458
00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:20.060
Well, I know something that was not expensive for Boo's Cruise.

459
00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:20.860
What?

460
00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:26.100
But the cans of soup that y'all had to use to throw up.

461
00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.220
That was not an expensive line item.

462
00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:29.740
No.

463
00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:31.240
And then it didn't even make it in.

464
00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:32.260
It didn't even make it in.

465
00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.760
We had a fake puke minestrone soup.

466
00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:35.120
Ugh.

467
00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:36.300
Anyway, sorry.

468
00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:43.080
Well, Kent also shared what he called the, quote, most expensive element of an episode.

469
00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:50.500
And he said the most surprisingly costly gag was in the business school episode.

470
00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.460
And it was the bat gag.

471
00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.760
He said CGI was kind of in its infancy.

472
00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.800
Prices were really costly back then.

473
00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:01.820
So animating that bat cost a lot of money.

474
00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.960
You know, we talk about this in our breakdown of business school.

475
00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:08.020
And it really was an elaborate undertaking.

476
00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:16.380
Because we had a live bat, two bat wranglers, a mechanical bat, and then all the CGI.

477
00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:18.220
Crazy.

478
00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:18.740
Yeah.

479
00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:20.820
It looks phenomenal when you watch it.

480
00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:21.700
Still to this day.

481
00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:21.920
Still.

482
00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:26.820
And then, Jenna, you know, your list that you talked about at the beginning that you thought

483
00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.380
was most expensive, Kent actually laid it out for us.

484
00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.580
Goodbye, Toby came in at number two.

485
00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:32.080
Okay.

486
00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:32.620
Okay.

487
00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:35.160
Beach Games was number three.

488
00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:40.500
And for a show on location, he said Beach Games was surprisingly inexpensive.

489
00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:46.300
The lake that we used as part of the LA parks and permit and location fees were not costly

490
00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:46.700
at all.

491
00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:51.680
The most expensive gag from that was probably the fire pit.

492
00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:53.440
Oh, yeah.

493
00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:59.340
I mean, are there any other big gags from that episode?

494
00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:00.100
Because you're right.

495
00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.700
We're all just kind of like out there in the sand.

496
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:04.420
Mm-hmm.

497
00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.040
I mean, I guess Ed floating in the water.

498
00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:10.320
But honestly, that was just like a boat and some rope.

499
00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.140
It was a tiny rowboat.

500
00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:16.120
And one guy in it, and they tethered Ed.

501
00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.540
They had a long rope, and they would just kind of push him out.

502
00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:20.140
Yeah.

503
00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:22.560
I was just talking to Oscar Nunez about this.

504
00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:24.080
He had just re-watched Beach Games.

505
00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:25.900
He goes, you can't even tell it's Ed.

506
00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:26.580
I know.

507
00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:27.940
It's like dark out there.

508
00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:31.380
Where he was floating in that water for so long.

509
00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:32.740
Like a really long time.

510
00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:33.500
Oh, Ed.

511
00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:34.660
Yeah.

512
00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.740
I mean, there's only like one or two moments where you can clearly see his face.

513
00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:41.020
There's so many things like that.

514
00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:46.060
So many practical things that I feel like actors today just don't have to do.

515
00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:49.080
I mean, when we were talking to those guys about the paper, they're like, oh, no, we

516
00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.740
don't drive cars on real roads.

517
00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:53.260
It's all like studio green screen.

518
00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:54.280
We're like, oh, really?

519
00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:55.180
Okay.

520
00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:59.600
For the four hours we were filming, they might have had a double for it.

521
00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:00.180
Exactly.

522
00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.500
Maybe it wasn't in our budget.

523
00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:02.820
All right, everyone.

524
00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.200
Let's move on to seasons five through seven.

525
00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:10.200
This is when Randy Cordray was our producer and Steve Burgess was our unit production manager.

526
00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.980
Well, you know, without even having to ask, I immediately thought the most expensive episode

527
00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:20.980
would have been Jim and Pam, the proposal from Weight Loss Part 2, because, you know, we built

528
00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:21.720
that whole thing.

529
00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.780
Yes, same, and we are correct.

530
00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:29.840
Randy said that the two-parter of Weight Loss was not only the first episode of his tenure,

531
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.520
but it was almost a half a million dollars over budget.

532
00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:35.520
Can you imagine how stressed you are?

533
00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.560
This is your first time working on the show.

534
